---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  SeaViews as SeaViewsViz,
  ComplexityMatrix,
} from "../../components/cave/sea-views";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="Buildings With Ocean View"
  description="Visualización con Phaser del barrido de derecha a izquierda"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Many people dream of living by the sea. But being close does not always
        mean having a view. This building is only a few steps from the beach.
        Yet it will never see the ocean. The problem is not distance. The
        problem is height.
      </p>
      <pre>Highlight building in index 2</pre>
    </aside>

    <SeaViewsViz client:load heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]} width={1080} height={720} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <!-- intro -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        A building has an ocean view if every building in front of it — toward
        the beach — is strictly shorter. If there is one building that is equal
        or taller, the view is blocked. That's all.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <SeaViewsViz client:load heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]} width={1080} height={500} />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Now, how would we check this? Suppose we want to know whether a single
        building has an ocean view. Let us pick the one furthest from the ocean.
        It has height four. In front of it, toward the ocean, we see buildings
        with heights 1, 3, and 2. All of them are shorter. So this building has
        a view.
      </p>
      <p>
        Now let us try another one. This building has height two. In front of
        it, toward the ocean, we see 1 and 3. And here we immediately find a
        problem. Three is taller than two. So this building is blocked.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex items-center justify-center h-full">
      <SeaViewsViz
        client:load
        heights={[1, 2, 7, 3, 6, 5, 8]}
        showEditor={true}
        width={1080}
        height={580}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        That works. But what if we want to know which buildings have a view? Not
        just one. All of them. The straightforward idea is simple. For each
        building, check all the buildings in front of it. Repeat the same
        process again and again.
      </p>
    </aside>
    1, 2 and 4th have views
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let us actually see what that means. We draw a matrix. Each column
        represents a building. Each row represents a comparison against a
        building in front of it. Every time we compare two buildings, we place a
        dot in the matrix.
      </p>
      <p>
        Let us start from the building closest to the ocean. It does not need to
        compare with anyone. The next one must compare with one building. The
        next one with two. And so on.
      </p>
      <p>
        In the worst case, we fill half of an n by n grid. That is about n
        squared divided by two comparisons. And that is why the time complexity
        is O of n squared.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <ComplexityMatrix
      client:load
      heights={[1, 3, 2, 4]}
      showEditor={false}
      width={1080}
      height={720}
    />
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>Veamos el código en Python.</p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def findBuildings(heights):
    max_right = -1
    answer = []

    for i in range(len(heights) - 1, -1, -1):
        if heights[i] > max_right:
            answer.append(i)
            max_right = heights[i]

    answer.reverse()
    return answer`}
    />
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
